  <h2>What mold actually does to health</h2>
  <p>The CDC describes mold's main effects as <strong>allergy and breathing symptoms</strong>: a stuffy nose, wheezing, and red or itchy eyes or skin. People with <strong>asthma</strong> or <strong>mold allergies</strong> can react more strongly, and people with <strong>weakened immune systems</strong> or <strong>chronic lung disease</strong> are at higher risk of infections. Exposure matters: more mold and longer time in the room means more chance of symptoms.</p>
  <p>That's why the answer to "is it safe tonight?" depends on <strong>who</strong> is sleeping there and <strong>how much</strong> mold there is, not on its color.</p>

  <h2>Tonight: a quick decision</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Sleep elsewhere tonight if…</th><th>Staying one more night is usually reasonable if…</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Anyone in the room has asthma, allergies, a weakened immune system or chronic lung disease</td><td>Healthy adults only</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The visible mold is larger than about 10 square feet</td><td>A small patch you can see the edges of</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It followed sewage or floodwater</td><td>It followed a clean-water leak</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A strong musty smell fills the room, or the wall or ceiling is still wet or sagging</td><td>Faint or no smell, surfaces dry</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Someone is already having symptoms</td><td>No one has symptoms</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Many families also choose to move babies and older relatives out until cleanup is done. That's a reasonable, low-cost precaution.</p>

  <h2>If you stay in the room tonight</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Don't disturb it.</strong> Scrubbing, scraping or dry-brushing releases spores. Leave it for tomorrow's proper cleanup.</li>
    <li><strong>Cover a small patch</strong> loosely with plastic sheeting and painter's tape if you can do it without rubbing the surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Move the bed</strong> away from the affected wall.</li>
    <li><strong>Ventilate</strong> if the outdoor air is dry, and keep the door to the rest of the house closed.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't run the central heating or air conditioning</strong> if you suspect mold in the ducts or air handler.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Do you need a mold test?</h2>
  <p>Usually not. The EPA says that if you can <strong>see or smell mold</strong>, sampling is usually unnecessary, and there are <strong>no EPA or federal limits</strong> for mold to compare results against. The CDC agrees you don't need to know the species: <strong>remove it and fix the moisture</strong>. Testing is useful in specific situations, like finding a hidden source when you smell mold but can't see it, or checking a remediation, and should be done by a qualified professional.</p>
  <p>Be wary of anyone who offers to test <em>and</em> remediate and uses scary results to sell the job. The size of the visible damage and the moisture source matter more than a lab report.</p>

  <h2>The next 48 hours</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Find and stop the water.</strong> Mold always has a moisture source: the leak you found, condensation, or a damp wall. Cleaning without fixing it means it comes back. See <a href="/guides/signs-of-hidden-water-damage/">hidden water damage</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Photograph it</strong> with something for scale, for your records and any insurance claim. Mold coverage is often limited; see <a href="/guides/does-homeowners-insurance-cover-water-damage/">what insurance covers</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Small, from clean water, no vulnerable people?</strong> Clean it yourself: detergent and water on hard surfaces, gloves, goggles and an N95. Porous materials like drywall, ceiling tiles and carpet with mold usually have to be removed. The EPA doesn't recommend bleach as a routine fix.</li>
    <li><strong>Larger, inside walls, in ducts, or after dirty water?</strong> Call a professional. Remediation under the IICRC S520 standard seals off the area so spores don't spread during removal.</li>
    <li><strong>Dry everything</strong> within 24 to 48 hours of any new wetting, and keep indoor humidity low.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When to see a doctor</h2>
  <p>If anyone has wheezing, trouble breathing, worsening asthma or symptoms that don't settle after leaving the room, contact a doctor. Tell them about the mold. For a medical emergency, call 911.</p>

